==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|mīlitia||army, military force/service}}, from {{m|la|mīles||soldier}}.

The use of "militia" rather than "police" to refer to the police force (of Ukraine and some other countries) originated among Russian communists.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/məˈlɪʃə/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-militia.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪʃə|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|in particular|lang=en}} An [[army]] of [[train]]ed [[civilian]]s, which may be an [[official]] [[reserve]] army, called upon in time of need, the entire [[able-bodied]] [[population]] of a state which may also be called upon{{,}} or a [[private]] force not under [[government]] [[control]].
# The national [[police force]] of certain countries (e.g. Ukraine).

